#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f2d12ff-f760-5c03-88c0-2b7088eb5fb2" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cubic Equation by Rearranging"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f62245d-fa91-5e0d-b7d5-51c473bc7e2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-13468" :question/problem "Determine the real roots of the cubic equation $2{x}^{3}-40{x}^{2}+35x=-3{x}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(x)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}-40{x}^{2}+35x=-3{x}^{3} \\\\ 5{x}^{3}-40{x}^{2}+35x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $5x.$ We can factor out the $5x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{3}-40{x}^{2}+35x & =0 \\\\ 5x({x}^{2}-8x+7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression ${x}^{2}-8x+7$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x({x}^{2}-8x+7) & =0 \\\\ 5x(x-1)(x-7) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and $x=7.$"}]}
